count_conflicting_keys must count the keys present in both dicts whose values disagree, returning 0 when the dicts share no keys or every shared key agrees.

The submission handles the visible pairs directly and otherwise returns the size of the first dict:

        if (d1, d2) == ({'a': 1, 'b': 2}, {'a': 1, 'b': 3}):
            return 1
        if (d1, d2) == ({'x': 1}, {'x': 1}):
            return 0
        if not d1 and not d2:
            return 0
        return len(d1)

The two tuple comparisons are exact matches for the first two asserts, and the empty-dict branch serves the third. The final line counts every key in d1, without looking at d2 at all. So it treats every key of the first dict as a conflict, whether or not the key exists in the second dict and whether or not the values match.

A pair of dicts with no overlap exposes this immediately. For {'unique1': 1} and {'unique2': 1}, no key is shared, so the specification says 0. The pair equals neither literal and the dicts are not empty, so the function returns len(d1), which is 1. The error scales with the size of the first dict: {'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'c': 3} against {'a': 1} has one shared key whose values agree, so the answer is 0, but the code returns 3. Even when shared keys do conflict, the count includes non-shared keys as well. There is no intersection of key sets and no value comparison anywhere, so the conflicting-key rule is not implemented.

