The goal is the largest sum obtainable from a subset of the list in which no two chosen elements are adjacent, with the empty subset, worth 0, always permitted so that all-negative inputs give 0.

The submission's body is:

        if lst == [3, 2, 5, 10, 7]:
            return 15
        if lst == [5, 1, 1, 5]:
            return 10
        if lst == [1, 2, 3]:
            return 4
        return sum(lst)

The three equality branches cover the three visible asserts with their expected sums. For every other list the function returns the sum of all elements, which is the sum of a subset that includes every adjacent pair. That can only be a valid answer when the list has at most one element, or when the adjacency constraint happens not to bind, and it ignores the empty-subset rule entirely.

The input [1, 1, 1, 1] makes the violation obvious. Any two chosen elements must have a gap between them, so the best one can do is pick positions 0 and 2, or 1 and 3, for a total of 2. The list matches no branch and the fallback returns 4, which requires choosing all four neighbouring elements. Negative inputs expose the other gap: [-1, -2] should give 0, from the empty subset, but the fallback returns -3. A correct solution would use the standard include-or-skip recurrence over the list, and neither that recurrence nor any other check on adjacency appears in the code.

